  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the quark content and decay mechanism of a neutral D meson into two pions. At the top, it introduces the relevant quark pairs and hadrons: a proton made of two up quarks and one down quark, and a \\(D^0\\) meson made of a charm quark and an anti-up quark. This sets up the particle identities used in the decay diagrams below.\n\nThe lower part compares two types of Feynman-like contributions to the same decay process, \\(D^0 \\to \\pi^- \\pi^+\\). In the “Tree” diagram, the charm quark decays through a \\(W\\) boson into lighter quarks, while the spectator anti-up quark continues into one of the final pions. The quark lines reorganize to form the outgoing \\(\\pi^-\\) and \\(\\pi^+\\).\n\nNext to it, the “Penguin” diagram shows a loop-mediated alternative. Here the charm quark again transitions via a \\(W^+\\), but the process includes an internal loop with down-type quarks labeled \\(d,s,b\\) and a gluon that helps generate the final quark pair. The comparison highlights that the same final state can arise from a direct weak decay or a more indirect loop process, which is the main scientific point of the figure.",
